  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nDAILY MOUNTAIN SHOWERS AND STORMS IN THE AFTERNOON: \n\nA shortwave trough is moving across the region today. This\nshortwave will provide some support for mountain showers and\nthunderstorms, primarily during the afternoon hours over the\nsouthern and central Colorado mountains. After today, afternoon\nshowers and storms remain in the forecast each day through\nSaturday. Substantial rainfall is not expected from this  \nconvective activity, but the majority of any measurable \nprecipitation is expected to occur today and tomorrow. High \ntemperatures will continue to be above normal each day through \nthe workweek, with highs forecast to be 5 to 15 degrees above \nnormal. \n\nPOTENTIAL FOR WIDESPREAD RAINFALL BEGINNING SUNDAY:\n\nA closed low looks to develop over the Pacific Northwest later\nthis week, moving southward along the West Coast and then\npotentially inland across the Intermountain West late this\nweekend or early next week. Guidance has consistently shown this\npattern shift between runs, and deterministic models are in \nrelatively good agreement about the initial development and\nmovement of the low. So, confidence has increased regarding \nthis pattern shift and the subsequent potential for widespread \nprecipitation and cooler temperatures in our region. However, \nconfidence regarding timing and impacts for our area is still \nlow at this point. Over the coming days confidence in the \nforecast for moisture and forcing will increase and potential \nimpacts will become more understood.",
